     I think what people are ignoring when they talk about 
class distinctions is not realism but play balance.  The 
fact is, even in AD&D, a mage can't get ANYWHERE without a 
few fighters to back him up.  (At first, anyway.)  It'd be 
like this in Nethack, too, if we restrict him to a cloak, 
rings and perhaps bracers (assuming we create some in order 
to be nice-- the standard rules are that bracers can be of 
AC 2 to 6 and do not combine with armor, e.g. wearing 
leather and bracers of AC 4 gives you AC 4; wearing plate 
and bracers of AC 6 gives you AC 3 for the plate) for armor 
and a dagger, darts and staff for weapons.
     Nethack is a single-player game as it stands.  Each 
and every player class needs to have the potential to 
overcome each and every obstacle in the game.  There's no 
way around that, except possible polymorphing your pet 
into the Wizard of Yendor.  (Oh, if only you could control 
your pets' polymorphs.  Too easyt.  You *can* assemble a 
party of humans as is by taming enough of them, but 
they're likely to get in your way quite a bit.  And 
although you can take up to 8 of them downstairs with you, 
good luck getting them to arrange like that.  Do magic 
whistles work on other than dogs?  And they'll consume a 
LOT of food....)
     I think class distinctions will become playable if 
and when:
a) Nethack develops a (hopefully quite unique) method of 
supporting multiple players at once.  I always thought 
this was a neat idea, if you assign one player to PC class 
and give him the possibility of "joining up" with others, 
whom he could order to do various things (perhaps up to 
three orders per turn, and some actions are long-term.  
Morale would also be a factor).  Monsters would have to 
appear in proportion to both party strength (as they 
currently do in proportion to character level, in size 
rather than number) and party number, so as to avoid 
starving a larger party or overwhelming a smaller one.  
The major problem I see (apart from code complexity) is 
that the levels are just too damn small to have six people 
walking around in a tight-nit group.  And having people 
wander all over the level (without being ordered-- orders 
could take place in a line-of-sight, BTW, not just 
adjacent) would be just like having so many human pets.  I 
don't like the idea of using a @ to represent the whole 
party on the map; it doesn't allow for much tactical 
movement and such.  Oh, and move_pet or move_NPC would be 
a necessity, regardless!  The main difficulty apart from 
that is getting NPCs to act on their own without being 
ordered.
     Morale would be a significant, but pretty 
straight-forward, part of the code.  Determine the NPC's 
"default reaction", come up with a number for difference 
between the two possible moves (attacking vs. fleeing would 
be the greatest, obviously; casting a spell, moving 
strategically, or other such moves would be in the middle 
of the spectrum), adjust for player charisma and current 
morale, and then perform an rn2() or rnd() on that 
number.  If it comes up greater than a certain constant, 
the current morale goes down slightly (if morale hits a 
certain level, they become a peaceful monster, I suppose, 
and if possible shouldn't fight with other members of the 
party) and they perform the default action.  This should 
only be likely to happen if the NPC wants to flee and is 
directed to attack, but would have a slight chance to 
happen, especially if the player's charisma (or luck!) was 
low, if the NPC wanted to cast a spell and was directed 
to fight hand-to-hand.  Having readied, say, a bow in the 
turn before would create a significant impetus to fire an 
arrow rather than switch back to a sword.  Killing a 
monster personally would increase morale, as would 
receiving items, etc.  NPCs would have to be ordered to 
organize their inventory (read spellbooks, etc.).  
Ordering them to surrender an item would decrease morale 
by the same factor that it was raised in giving it to him, 
and would entail a morale check as well, although there 
might be a mechanism for "trading" items so that giving 
him Mjollnir and having him surrender Stormbringer would 
not entail a morale check.

2) Magic be significantly improved and developed.
3) A mechanism be developed for affecting both monster and 
object distribution, both for multiple players and class 
focus.

     In short, it'd require a lot of work, and it probably 
would show up in Nethack 3.1.  Comments are welcome.



